Under X, each route pays 39 in 4 of the 8 equally likely states and 7 in the other 4. Either route therefore has expected value (39+7)/2 = 23. Since you get no information about the state, you cannot improve on 23 by choosing between R1 and R2.

Under Y, the payoff is a certain 25, which is higher than 23.

{"choice":"Y"}

Under X, each route pays 39 in four of the eight equally likely states and 7 in the other four. Either route therefore has expected value (39 + 7) / 2 = 23. Option Y pays 25 for certain, which is higher than 23.

Because the state is only revealed after the route is executed, there is no way to learn it first and pick the better route. Y has the higher expected value.

{"choice":"Y"}

Under Y, the states are equally likely and I learn the exact state before picking a repair. In S1, S2, S3 and S5 both repairs pay 18, and in S4, S6, S7 and S8 both pay 2. So 4 of 8 states pay 18 and 4 of 8 pay 2.

The expected value of Y is 0.5 × 18 + 0.5 × 2 = 10. X pays 11 for certain. Since 11 > 10, X is better.

{"choice":"X"}

Every route pays the same in each state, so the choice of route does not matter. The expected value of entering the process is (3 + 43 + 3 + 43) / 4 = 23.

Option X pays 35 for certain, which is more than 23, so X is better.

{"choice":"X"}

Routes R1 and R2 give identical payoffs in every state. Four states pay 39 (S1, S4, S6, S8) and four pay 7 (S2, S3, S5, S7). Since the states are equally likely, the expected value of X is (39 + 7) / 2 = 23.

Y pays 25 for certain, and 25 > 23, so Y is better.

{"choice":"Y"}

The table shows every repair gives the same payoff in a given state: 20 in S1 and S2, and 0 in S3 and S4. Because the choice of repair makes no difference, the expected value of X is 0.5 × 20 = 10.

Y gives 16 for certain, which is more than 10.

{"choice":"Y"}

Entering the process (X) lets you learn the exact state before you pick a repair. In every state, at least one repair pays 20: S1 has R1 and R2, S2 has R1 and R4, S3 has R3 and R4, and S4 has R2 and R3. So X gives 20 points for certain, which beats the 16 from Y.

The one thing to check is whether "you learn the exact state BEFORE choosing the repair" really applies after you enter. It does, as the problem states, so the best repair is available in every state.

{"choice":"X"}

Each route pays 43 in two of the four states and 3 in the other two. Since the states are equally likely, every route has expected value (43 + 43 + 3 + 3) / 4 = 23.

Option X pays 35 for certain, which is more than 23. With no information about the state at any point, no route choice can beat 23, so Y cannot do better than that.

{"choice":"X"}

Under Y, every route pays the same amount within a given state, so learning the state doesn't change the payoff. The payoff is 3 in S1 and S3 and 43 in S2 and S4. Each state has probability 1/4, so the expected value of Y is (3 + 43 + 3 + 43)/4 = 23.

X pays 35 for certain, and 35 is greater than 23.

{"choice":"X"}

Both routes pay the same in every state, so the report has no effect on which route I pick. The expected value of entering is (27 + 3 + 3 + 27) / 4 = 15.

Y gives 19 for certain, and 19 > 15, so Y is better.

{"choice":"Y"}

Option Y: after entering, you learn the exact state before choosing the repair, so you can pick the better repair in every state and get 18 points each time.

Expected value of Y = 18, compared with 11 for X.

{"choice":"Y"}
